FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/analyzer.rs:216 — `analyze_deferred_body` never checks `manifest_failed`, so an `import` inside a function/method/object/if/loop/try body still walks the rest of that body after recording the manifest conflict — the "page of consequences" this run claimed to eliminate returns for every nested import — no test covers an import inside any body — stop the walk inside `analyze_deferred_body` and the scoped branches (as the top-level loop at src/analyzer.rs:85 does) instead of only breaking the top-level statement loop.
2. [MAJOR] src/manifest.rs:473 — `MAX_RESOLUTION_DEPTH` is enforced as fan-out width (`module_manifest.dependencies.len() > MAX_RESOLUTION_DEPTH`), not chain depth, while `depth` at src/manifest.rs:397 only counts popped steps against `MAX_RESOLUTION_STEPS` — a 100-deep single-dependency chain passes despite the documented "how deep one module's chain may go" at src/manifest.rs:72 — track chain depth per queued edge (store depth with the `pending` entry) and refuse past 64.
3. [MAJOR] src/bytecode/vm.rs:580 — `module_paths` still resolves via cwd-cached `pinned_module_path`, while the analyzer and tree-walker helpers now resolve via root-aware `pinned_module_path_in` (src/interpreter.rs:417) — `analyze_in(root)` and bytecode execution disagree whenever root != cwd, and the cached path is never exercised against a scratch root by any test — thread the same `root` (or VFS root) into the bytecode loader instead of leaving it on `.`.
4. [MAJOR] src/manifest.rs:642 — `pinned_module_path` caches the first `resolve_in(".")` Ok-or-Err string per thread forever and never invalidates — a second program/manifest in the same process (REPL, harness) gets the first program's pins or its failure, while `failure_in` re-resolves fresh, so analyzer and loader diverge in-process — key the cache by canonical root plus manifest/lock mtime, or drop the cache.
5. [MAJOR] src/analyzer.rs:428 — every `Import` statement calls `failure_in(&self.root)`, and `failure_in`/`pinned_module_path_in` both go through writing `resolve_in` (src/manifest.rs:594 writes via `write_lockfile`) — N imports means N full directory-scan resolves plus N lockfile writes, so `rb lint`/`rb format`/`analyze_in` create `redblue.lock` as a side effect — add a read-only `resolve_without_writing` for static passes and resolve once per `analyze` call, not once per import.
6. [MAJOR] src/manifest.rs:98 — every manifest parse/resolve error is built with `Span::unknown()` (same at src/manifest.rs:142, src/manifest.rs:208 and all siblings), so `Error::span()` is `None` and the manifest line lives only inside the message string; the analyzer then stores `error.to_string()` ("RuntimeError: ...") at src/analyzer.rs:429 and re-wraps it as `Analyzer`, producing the reported `AnalyzerError: RuntimeError: Cannot resolve ...` double prefix pointing at the import instead of the manifest line — keep the structured message without the embedded label and attach the manifest file:line (or the import span without re-embedding the label).
